WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.350
DAVID HAIKNEY: Hello, everybody,

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.830
and welcome to this online
video for Microsoft Build.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.520
I'm David Haikney, Technical
Product Director at Arm.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.740
In this short video, I'll
walk you through how

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.430
to use our new performance
analysis toolkit, Arm Performix,

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.660
to help you observe

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.330
and accelerate the workloads
running on Cobalt.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.610
Microsoft and Arm have an
incredibly strong partnership,

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.490
and Performix has been developed
in close collaboration

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.430
with Microsoft
performance experts.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.830
I know many of you may
be migrating to Cobalt

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.660
for the first time,
and so I wanted

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.610
to briefly draw your
attention to some

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.250
of the fabulous resources to
help you on your journey.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.960
Before jumping into the demo,
I want to take a moment

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.390
to set the scene on
performance analysis.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.830
Performance analysis
can be thought

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.630
of like a crime investigation.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.120
You have suspects,
leads, hypotheses.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.950
You need to gather
evidence to help support

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.570
or disprove your theories.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.700
There may be blind
alleys and dead ends.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.180
New evidence may require you to
revisit previous assumptions.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.870
It is not a straightforward,
linear process.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.140
Performance engineers need to
wrangle a very wide, complex,

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.180
and fragmented toolset.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.720
That is why we built
Arm Performix.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.440
Whilst each investigation is
unique, the overall approach

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.850
that expert performance analysts
take tends to be consistent.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.430
Investigations tend
to start very broad

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.100
and acquire an overall
picture before diving deeper

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.690
into the details.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.200
Experiments are run with good
science, changing one variable

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.130
at a time, repeating the runs
to understand consistency,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.970
and comparing results
against previous baselines.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.240
Specifically, this means
starting with an understanding

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.310
of what the platform,
in our case,

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.790
the Cobalt instance,
is capable of.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.970
Then, understanding how
this system behaves

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.100
when the workload is applied.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.360
This will help dictate where
analysis is best focused.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.860
We'll show you some examples

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.230
of where we can go deeper
with the analysis.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.490
So let's jump into the demo.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.660
This is the Performix UI.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.450
Everything we'll demo here is
also available via the command

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.380
line for those that prefer to do
the analysis from the terminal.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.300
To get started,
I'll introduce you

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.070
to two key concepts,
recipes and targets.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.440
Targets are the systems,
again, in our case,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.120
the Cobalt instances, that we're
going to run our workload on.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.130
Adding a target is a simple case

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:37.110
of supplying the relevant SSH
credentials, host, user, port,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.730
and any required SSH keys.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.760
The next concept is recipes.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.240
These are the experiments
or workflows we want to run

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.240
on our target as part of
our performance analysis.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.640
There are a number
of recipes here.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.980
Those that are still under
development are marked

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.880
as experimental as we continue
to expand this toolkit.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.410
We'll start by assessing our
overall Cobalt target instance.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.370
We do this by running the
system characterization recipe.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:12.090
This is going to run a series of
microbenchmarks on the platform.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.250
Running a recipe, we
select the target,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.860
choose the benchmarks
we want to run.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.260
Performix makes sure
the target is set up

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.090
and any dependencies
are installed

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.690
on the target system
on your behalf.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.230
Here's an example of one of the
system characterization outputs,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.370
how memory bandwidth varies with
access size, or in other words,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.200
how the cache is performing.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.640
This recipe is useful

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.340
to validate the instance
itself is performing

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.530
at the expected level.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.900
The next step is to
see how it behaves

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.090
when the workload is running.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.320
We can do this with the
system utilization recipe.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.770
With this recipe, we can choose
to launch a new workload,

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.110
attach to an already
running process,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.600
or profile across all processes.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.540
In this example, I focus on
the CPU of our 96-core system.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.990
This is a heat map of
how busy each core is

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.300
over a one-second interval.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.430
We can see that
roughly two-thirds

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.110
of the cores are occupied

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.140
and the remaining
third relatively idle.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.800
This at-a-glance
visualization generates lots

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.500
of potential avenues
for our investigations.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.570
Perhaps we can increase
the thread count,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.310
maybe a different instance size,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.890
or perhaps there's some lock
contention that's occupying

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.060
these cores.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.080
Let's dig deeper into what those
cores are actually running.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.260
We can go deeper using
the code hotspots recipe.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.080
This samples where time is
being spent in the code,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.130
and we can tie this back
to the source code itself.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.890
This recipe works
across all languages,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.210
including Java and.NET.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.950
Understanding which parts

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.060
of our code are hot ensures we
can spend time optimizing the

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.790
right places, something
that can't be done

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.610
with static analysis tools.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.720
Another way we can dig

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.830
into where time is being spent
is using instruction mix.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.280
This is useful for understanding
how well the application is

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.400
taking advantage of the
platform's capabilities.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.530
I mentioned earlier
how it's often useful

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.010
to compare the results of
one experiment with another.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.040
Performix allows you to
easily compare between runs.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.960
This example shows the
different instruction mixes

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.810
between two different
matrix multiplications,

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:33.180
one using Arm NEON instructions,
the other using SVE.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.820
And we can go deeper
still using the CPU

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.640
microarchitecture recipe.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.720
This uses Arm's top-down
methodology to characterize

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.310
where time is being spent

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.220
at various different
parts of the CPU.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.430
We can dig into how the various
layers of caching are performing

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.770
as well as detecting
pipeline stalls due

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.500
to branch mispredictions.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.680
As you can see, Performix
has the ability to do

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.250
that breadth-first analysis
across the entire system,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.100
and then go deep into
specific aspects

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.670
as the investigation unfolds.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.710
For the final part of
the demonstration,

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.570
we're going to look at
delivering insights

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.200
and actual improvements
from this analysis.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.570
Performix provides an MCP server

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.630
so that the analysis can be
tightly coupled alongside

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.310
an LLM.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:26.160
Here, next to my code
in Visual Studio,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.280
I'm able to summon the
analysis from all of the runs

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.380
that we just performed.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:35.100
Providing this profiling
data, disassembly,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.250
information about the target
system and source code

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.470
to the LLM allows Performix
to deliver dynamic insight

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.020
to suggest confidence-based
improvements

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.500
to accelerate the workload
running on the Cobalt platform.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:57.090
So in summary, you've seen
how Performix uses targets

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:00.750
and recipes to apply that
guided analysis approach

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:04.440
to our performance
investigation, starting broad

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.800
with system characterization
and utilization

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.620
and enabling deeper
analysis with a selection

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.080
of specialized recipes.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.670
Powerful MCP server integration
means performance analysis goes

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.790
from being a passive activity
of sampling and profiling

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:24.510
to a dynamic one of actively
suggesting improvements

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.770
and optimizations to
accelerate your workload.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:33.540
Performix is available now, free
to download and free to use.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.200
We'd love for you to try it out
and let us know your experience.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.070
Thank you for watching.
